bankruptcy is only for companies that  can't pay their bills right  wrong johnson and johnson despite having  billions in the bank and a perfect  credit rating put one of its units into  bankruptcy in order to protect itself  from billions of dollars in potential  litigation claims from people who say  they got sick from baby powder and it's  not alone america has a long tradition  of allowing profitable companies  to put units into bankruptcy to clear  away litigation claims the latest legal  strategy is called the texas two-step  and it works like this in step one a  company moves its corporate charter to  texas where a business friendly law  allows it to split in two with a good  company holding the healthy assets and  businesses while a bad company is  responsible for paying off legal claims  the bad company faces all the litigation  claims like the baby powder lawsuits  that johnson johnson faces for step two  the bad company files for bankruptcy  putting all current and future  litigation on hold until it can  negotiate a plan to pay everything off  the good company still has to kick in  some amount of money to help out  but it isn't in bankruptcy and its  credit rating is not ever threatened it  also benefits from the litigation freeze  potentially saving it millions of  dollars every month in legal fees jnj  did this in north carolina where two  judges have ruled for now that the  process is legitimate why is this legal  well if you've ever had to co-sign for a  car loan say for your college age  daughter then you know the answer if you  refuse to sign then your kid is on their  own if they can't pay the debt  the same principle applies in corporate  law the bad company is considered a  separate entity responsible for its own  debts courts have generally held but a  corporate parent is not responsible for  those debts  if it doesn't agree in advance the texas  two-step applies this principle to  lawsuits that could cost corporations  billions of dollars  [Music]
 

